Chapter: BuiltinAlloc
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 What does one call to a list builtin retain, and does that answer change
 when the input gets bigger? That is the measurement CostModel.md 5.1 names
 as the block on the `fixed` rung: separating "fixed bytes per call" from
 "one walk over an input" cannot be done from the code's shape, because a
 body with no loop in it can still call something that copies its argument.
 3.1 published the text family and nothing else. This is the list family.

 THE DISCRIMINATOR IS INPUT SIZE, NOT ITERATION COUNT, which is what makes
 this instrument different from `accumulator-corpus` and `literal-alloc`.
 Those run a loop n times and read the growth. Here every arm makes ONE
 call, and the two readings differ only in how large the argument is. A
 builtin that retains the same bytes on a list of 64 and a list of 256 is
 `fixed`; one that retains four times as much is walking its input.

 `list-push` GETS TWO ARMS AND THAT IS THE POINT. `__list_snoc` has three
 paths (`DevelopersGuide.md`, "list-push is NOT unconditionally
 destructive"): store in spare capacity, extend in place when the list is
 the topmost allocation, or COPY to a fresh allocation. Which one runs is
 positional, decided by the call site's allocation order and enforced by
 nothing. A cost class has to be the worst case over structure, so the
 buried arm -- one small allocation made after the list, which is all it
 takes -- is the arm that decides the class. Reading only the topmost arm
 would publish `fixed` for a builtin that copies.

 The control arm makes no call at all, so the harness's own retention is
 visible and every row is read against it rather than against zero.

 We say:

Section: The Instrument

  keep : List Integer -> Integer
  keep (xs) = list-length xs

  mk-list : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mk-list (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else mk-list n (i + 1) (list-push acc i)

  ratio-tenths : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ratio-tenths (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n <= 0 then 0
    else (at-4n * 10) / at-n

  show-tenths : Integer -> Text
  show-tenths (t) = show (t / 10) & "." & show (t - (t / 10) * 10)

 The class this instrument can report, and it deliberately cannot report
 `growing`: one call cannot be nested in a walk, so the top of the lattice
 is not a question a single call answers.

  klass : Integer, Integer -> Text
  klass (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n == 0 & at-4n == 0 then "none"
    else if at-n == at-4n then "fixed"
    else "input"

  row : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (name) (at-n) (at-4n) =
    name & " n=" & show at-n & " 4n=" & show at-4n
      & " x" & show-tenths (ratio-tenths at-n at-4n)
      & " " & klass at-n at-4n

Section: Arms

  m-control : Integer -> Integer
  m-control (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-length : Integer -> Integer
  m-length (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-at xs (n / 2)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-push-topmost : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-topmost (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 One three-element list built after the input is enough to move it off the
 top, which is the whole of the aliasing rule in the guide.

  m-push-buried : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-buried (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 THE ARM THAT SHOWS THIS INSTRUMENT CAN SAY "CHEAP". `base` is 64 and 256,
 both powers of two, and `mk-list` leaves capacity equal to length there, so
 the very next push always lands on the doubling boundary and pays the whole
 capacity. That is the worst case and it is the right number for a CEILING,
 but an instrument that can only ever report it is not measuring, it is
 asserting. One extra element puts the list at length n+1 in a backing array
 of 2n, and the same call then takes the spare-capacity path.

  m-push-spare : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-spare (n) =
    let xs = mk-list (n + 1) 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-set-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-set-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-set-at xs (n / 2) 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-insert-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-insert-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-insert-at xs (n / 2) 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-tail : Integer -> Integer
  m-tail (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (__list-tail xs) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Text Family

 The published Text table (`DevelopersGuide.md`, 2026-08-15) measured each of
 these at ONE input size, which is enough to say "it allocates" and not enough
 to say which class it is. `substring` reading "16 bytes per call at n = 4" is
 the case in point: 16 bytes is a fact about that call, and whether it is a
 CEILING depends on which of the two lengths it tracks.

 SO SUBSTRING GETS TWO ARMS, VARYING DIFFERENT THINGS, and the pair is the
 finding rather than either number. One holds the OUTPUT at four characters and
 grows the input; the other takes half the input and grows both. If the cost
 follows the output, a caller taking a fixed-size field out of a line of any
 length is `fixed` and can say so, which is most of the parsing in this tree.
 If it follows the input, none of them can.

  mk-text : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  mk-text (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else mk-text n (i + 1) (acc & "x")

  m-text-length : Integer -> Integer
  m-text-length (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length s
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-sub-fixed-out : Integer -> Integer
  m-sub-fixed-out (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (substring s 0 4) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-sub-half-out : Integer -> Integer
  m-sub-half-out (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (substring s 0 (n / 2)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-contains : Integer -> Integer
  m-contains (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if text-contains s "xx" then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-starts : Integer -> Integer
  m-starts (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if text-starts-with s "xx" then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-compare : Integer -> Integer
  m-compare (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let t = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-compare s t
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-itoa : Integer -> Integer
  m-itoa (n) =
    let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (integer-to-text n) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 `integer-to-text`'s input size is DIGITS, and the arm above varies its
 argument from 64 to 256, which is two digits to three. That is not a 4x
 spread in the thing that could matter, so on its own it would be a `fixed`
 claim resting on almost no range. This one runs the fourth power: 8 digits
 against 10, and read together with the arm above it covers 2, 3, 8 and 10.

  m-itoa-big : Integer -> Integer
  m-itoa-big (n) =
    let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (integer-to-text (n * n * n * n)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-char-to-text : Integer -> Integer
  m-char-to-text (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (char-to-text (char-at s 0)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Entry

  base : Integer = 64

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (row "control       " (m-control base) (m-control (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "list-length   " (m-length base) (m-length (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "list-at       " (m-at base) (m-at (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "push-topmost  " (m-push-topmost base) (m-push-topmost (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "push-buried   " (m-push-buried base) (m-push-buried (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "push-spare    " (m-push-spare base) (m-push-spare (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "list-set-at   " (m-set-at base) (m-set-at (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "list-insert-at" (m-insert-at base) (m-insert-at (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "__list-tail   " (m-tail base) (m-tail (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "text-length   " (m-text-length base) (m-text-length (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "sub-fixed-out " (m-sub-fixed-out base) (m-sub-fixed-out (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "sub-half-out  " (m-sub-half-out base) (m-sub-half-out (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "text-contains " (m-contains base) (m-contains (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "text-starts   " (m-starts base) (m-starts (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "text-compare  " (m-compare base) (m-compare (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "integer-to-tex" (m-itoa base) (m-itoa (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "itoa-big      " (m-itoa-big base) (m-itoa-big (base * 4)))
    print-line-uni (row "char-to-text  " (m-char-to-text base) (m-char-to-text (base * 4)))
  end
